  "full_response": "It seems like your question might be a bit unclear or incomplete. If you are asking about right-of-way rules and who must yield under Kenyan traffic law, I can provide some general guidance:\n\n1. **General Rule**: Vehicles, including bicycles, are usually required to yield to pedestrians at crosswalks and ensure safe passage.\n\n2. **Right-of-Way in Intersections**:\n   - At T-intersections, traffic on the through road generally has the right-of-way.\n   - At Four-way intersections without a stop line or traffic signals, vehicles from the right typically have the right-of-way.\n\n3. **Yielding Situations**:\n   - Vehicles must yield to pedestrians at zebra or pedestrian crossings.\n   - Larger vehicles like trucks and buses must often allow smaller, more maneuverable vehicles like motorcycles to merge or move first in some scenarios.\n   - Drifters (sleek racing bicycles in Kenya) and motorcyclists often use specific rules that prioritize quick movement in traffic.\n\n4. **Emergencies**: Emergency vehicles usually have the right-of-way, and other drivers should yield.\n\nIf you have a specific scenario in mind or need more detailed information, please provide more details!",
